<decompilertest>
<!--
    linuxsyscall: render a 32-bit Linux `int 0x80` as the syscall it is, instead of
    an indirect call through the `swi` userop.  x86 SLEIGH lowers `INT imm8` to
    `intloc = swi(imm8); call [intloc]` (ia.sinc:3671) - correct for a general
    software interrupt, but on 32-bit Linux the vector and the ABI are both fixed,
    and the generic lowering costs the reader the syscall number AND every
    argument: the register writes that set them up lose their last reader and the
    dead-code fixpoint collects them.

    Stage model: S2, a classification of a lifted call by what the target platform
    does with the vector - the family `funcboundflow` / `cleanupcode` /
    `noreturn_extern` sit in.  P0 assertion: `option linuxsyscall on|off`.

    Layout (x86:LE:32, flat at 0x100000):
      start @ 0x100000 :  mov  $4,%eax          ; SYS_write
                          mov  $1,%ebx          ; fd
                          mov  $0x1234,%ecx     ; buf
                          mov  $0xd,%edx        ; count
                          int  $0x80
                          mov  $1,%eax          ; SYS_exit
                          mov  $7,%ebx          ; status
                          int  $0x80
                          ret

    The four `mov` argument setups are the load-bearing part of the test.  The pass
    runs in the PRE-SSA window, before ActionFuncLink, and gives the call spec a
    LOCKED input prototype over the ABI registers - so ActionFuncLink materializes
    the argument Varnodes and those writes keep a reader.  Recognizing the site any
    later would name the call and still print it with no arguments, because by then
    the setup is already gone.

    Pass 1 (default, linuxsyscall off - the pre-feature rendering): two
    `(*(code *)swi(0x80))()` indirect calls, and not one of the four constants
    survives.
    Pass 2 (option linuxsyscall on): `sys_write(1,0x1234,0xd)` and `sys_exit(7)`.

    Asserts #1/#2 pin the two renderings against each other; #3/#4 prove the
    argument setup survives ONLY in the on pass (each constant appears exactly
    once across the two passes); #5 pins the per-syscall arity - `exit` takes one
    argument, so `ecx`/`edx`, which still hold 0x1234 and 0xd at the second site,
    must NOT be printed as arguments to it.
-->
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:gcc">
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
b804000000bb01000000b934120000ba0d000000cd80b801000000bb07000000cd80c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="start"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu start</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option linuxsyscall on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="LINUXSYSCALL #1: both int 0x80 sites render as an indirect call through swi() only in the linuxsyscall-off pass" min="2" max="2">\*\)swi\(0x80\)\)\(\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LINUXSYSCALL #2: with the option on, the number in EAX names the call and the ABI registers become its arguments" min="1" max="1">sys_write\(1,0x1234,0xd\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LINUXSYSCALL #3: the second site resolves independently of the first (EAX reloaded between them)" min="1" max="1">sys_exit\(7\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LINUXSYSCALL #4: the pre-SSA seam is what keeps the argument setup alive - 0x1234 exists only in the on pass" min="1" max="1">0x1234</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="LINUXSYSCALL #5: arity comes from the syscall table, so exit's one argument does not absorb the stale ecx/edx" min="0" max="0">sys_exit\(7,</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
